00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.580
Last month, Elon Musk unveiled a
curiously anticipated Tesla truck.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.420
A vehicle that most people 
will never step inside or drive.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.000
Yet it was met with hysterical screaming and cheers.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.920
The power of Elon Musk to generate hype for his next project is the envy of CEOs across the world.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.580
So, what is the big deal about a truck,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.520
and why is creating a battery-powered truck 
such an impressive feat?

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.540
In the U.S. alone, 
the trucking industry contributes to about

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.480
23% of the total greenhouse gas emissions,

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.960
equating to 1.475 million metric tons of carbon dioxide.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.180
Converting this industry to renewable energy sources

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.760
would be a huge win 
in our battle against climate change.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.560
But this isn't just a matter 
of throwing some batteries in a truck,

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.840
adding some motors, and marketing it 
to bejesus using Elon Musk's hyper powers.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.440
There are actual technical issues 
we need to overcome first:

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.540
the most prevalent of which 
is the energy density of the batteries,

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.580
which is the amount of energy provided 
per kilogram of their own weight.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.260
Vehicles like semi-trucks 
are categorized as Class A vehicles,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.780
bound to a maximum weight of 36 tons 
by the Federal Highway Administration.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.640
This weight includes the weight 
of the truck and payload,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.060
so any increase in the truck weight

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.680
will result in a decrease in payload– 
the part that pays the bills.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:17.280
For an energy source like batteries,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.200
which have a low energy density 
when compared to fossil fuels,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.460
this creates issues when 
attempting to create a vehicle

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.440
that will offer a value proposition 
to the trucking industry.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.600
Let's assume our truck, without batteries, 
will weigh about 7 tons.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.320
This is based on current trucks without an engine.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.320
That leaves us 29 tons to play with

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.720
to figure out how much 
of our weight to assign to batteries,

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.620
and this is where designers need to make a decision:

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.260
include more batteries, 
and the truck will have a greater range,

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.280
but will be able to carry less payload.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.220
Or create a smaller battery pack, 
which sacrifices range for a greater payload.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:51.480
A diesel truck can carry over 20 tons 
with a range of 1,500 km, or 900 mi.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.060
We know Tesla will be creating two variants of its truck: 
a 300-mile and a 500-mile variant.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.440
And while Elon hyped the 0-60 acceleration 
that no truck driver is ever going to use

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.040
unless they want to be fired 
the moment they arrive at the depot,

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.080
they left out the one crucial bit of information 
everyone in the trucking industry was waiting for:

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.560
the weight of the empty truck, which will determine
exactly how much cargo the truck can carry,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.680
and thus how quickly a buyer 
will get a return on investment.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.860
I found the purposeful secrecy 
behind this information incredibly suspicious,

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.360
but it may just be a case 
of Elon wanting to wait another 2-3 years,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.120
with possible energy density improvements 
on the horizon, to make that announcement.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.100
The continued improvement of energy density 
was the driver of this new era of electrical vehicles,

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.320
and Tesla has been on the vanguard 
of many of the technological advancements,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.160
and it's in their best interest to do so.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:45.499
There really isn't any proprietary technology in Tesla that someone like BMW or Ford couldn't copy,

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:47.140
and quite frankly, improve upon.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:51.680
Tesla, without their own battery production,
is really just a very well-marketed brand.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.740
So let's calculate the energy these batteries 
will need to provide these two trucks,

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.860
and more importantly, how much they will weigh.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.220
To do this, we first need to estimate how much energy
this truck will consume for a given range.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.600
This is relatively easy. 
"Energy" is simply given as "power" by 'time".

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.040
We can calculate "time" by dividing the range 
by the average speed.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.400
And "power" equals "force" times "velocity".

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.980
We can now swap in the power components 
for this equation we made earlier.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:20.000
And, voila, we have our equation 
for the battery capacity needed for the truck.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:20.680
Okay,

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.780
jokes aside, this an equation taken 
from an absolutely fantastic paper

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.380
by Shashank Sripad
and another lad who needs less syllables in his name,

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.300
which goes into depth in this problem.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.500
But I'll give you a quick rundown of its components.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.220
To adequately calculate the force acting on the vehicle,

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.420
we first need to calculate the force 
required to overcome inertia,

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.140
that is, how much force 
it will take to get the vehicle moving,

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.260
which is this part of the equation.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.440
This takes into account the efficiency 
of the electric engines and brakes

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.280
and the energy recovered from regenerative braking.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:52.360
Now, we need to calculate 
the forces acting to slow the vehicle down

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.860
such as drag, the rolling friction on the wheels,
and gravity as the vehicle travels uphill.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.760
Musk specified that the range for the vehicle 
was calculated on a flat road,

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.200
so this component goes to "zero".

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.240
So if you work on mountainous roads, 
this truck may not be for you.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:10.440
From the event last month, we know that the truck 
has an astounding coefficient of drag of 0.36-

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.320
that's slippier than a Bugatti Chiron-

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.920
thanks to the lack of air intakes to cool the engine.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:19.500
We will take the mean rolling resistance 
of the truck tires at 0.0063,

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:21.980
and the average speed at 50mph

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:23.700
(this accounts for city driving too),

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:28.360
and with the average acceleration 
and deceleration at 0.112 m/s²,

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.880
and a frontal area of 7.2 m².

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.420
The rest are constants that you don't particularly 
need to worry about for this example,

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.300
but you can check 
the full calculations in the description

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.700
where I've included 
an interactive website that we created

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:43.400
so you can easily change these variables yourself 
to see how they affect the vehicle.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:49.600
With this tool we can see that the 300-mile variant 
would need a 550-600 kWh batteries,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.020
and the 500-mile variant would require 900-1000 kWh.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:59.080
With current batteries' energy densities and prices, 
these would weigh 4.7 and 7.9 tonnes,

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:02.900
and cost $108,000 and $180,000 respectively.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.880
These are the two most important metrics
for any company considering buying this truck,

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.780
as it determines the exact return-on-investment time.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:14.640
The batteries for the 500-mile version are going to be more expensive than a traditional truck alone

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.500
while having a shorter range 
and smaller hauling capacity.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:20.060
That's going to make 
the return-on-investment time longer,

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:24.820
even if the operating cost per mile is 
20% lower than a conventional diesel semi-truck.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.760
But profitability is not everything in this world.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.760
This truck will use approximately 
25% the energy of a conventional truck,

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.680
thanks to the extremely efficient engines,

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:36.500
and much of this will be provided by renewable energy.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:41.700
This truck has the potential to drastically reduce 
the greenhouse gas emissions of the trucking industry.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:46.360
Tesla leading the way on this technology 
and ignoring skeptics like me, while risking bankruptcy

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:50.900
for the sake of improving the planet is exactly 
why I admire Elon Musk so much in the first place.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:55.520
Honestly, I came into this video 
expecting the truck to be a deeply flawed technology,

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.660
After the event, I ranted on Twitter 
about how suspicious it was

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:01.200
that Musk didn't unveil the empty weight of the vehicle.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.380
I honestly thought the whole event 
was created to stir up hype

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.200
and generate funds for 
a cash-poor company through pre-orders.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.660
I expected my calculations 
to confirm my preconceived ideas,

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.900
but I've never been so glad to be wrong.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.540
There are many other factors that will affect this truck,

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:19.480
but you can investigate them on our site at  battery.real.realengineering

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:25.280
We can see how adding a 5% grade hill for 5% of the journey will increase the battery capacity required

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:28.800
for the 500-mile range by more than 200 kWh

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.940
and the weight of the truck by almost 2 tonnes

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:33.900
and increase the price by $44,000.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.960
Go ahead and play around with the tool yourself, 
and if you found it useful

